From 80bdaca73bf070373ac410a66d4f503767b222ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AngadSC <104170661+AngadSC@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:01:06 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] PWML importer: match biological states exactly (an omitted
 component means nil, not any value)

Problem
PwmlParser#store_object looks an incoming entity up with klass.find_by(attribute_hash).
attribute_hash comes from to_hash_shallow, which drops every blank element, so a
biological state whose tissue, cell type or subcellular location is omitted or
nil="true" has no key for that column. find_by then treats the column as a wildcard
and the state is bound to the first existing biological state that agrees on the
components that were given, e.g. a (species, location) state binds to a
(species, tissue, location) sibling. The state loses its identity: it is not created,
every element that referenced it is attached to the wrong state, and two states that
are different in the file collapse into one row. This contradicts the model, which
defines a state by its exact tuple: BiologicalState validates uniqueness of each
component in the scope of the other three (nil is matched as IS NULL) and
BiologicalState.find_existing does an exact where.

Evidence (local import harness, empty database, Rails 4.2 / Ruby 2.2, importer before
and after this change plus the 10-line element-id change that is uncommitted in the
owner checkout; same 39 PWML files imported twice)
- Real PathBank exports (PW012926, PW741182, PW176888): states are written with
  nil="true" components and a pwbs-id that is shared by many states (PW_BS000024 is on
  32 states of PW741182), so pwbs_id does not make the lookup exact. Biological states
  created, before -> after: 262 -> 273, 176 -> 183, 342 -> 351, which is exactly the
  number of states in each file (273, 183, 351). The 7, 11 and 9 missing rows were
  states collapsed into siblings. PW687775: 18 -> 18. No other created count and no
  message changed in any of the four.
- Files written by the T2PW writer (no pwbs-id, omitted components): 7 of 20 files
  with wildcard-bindable states change (3, 10, 119, 120, 146, 150, 151), 13 do not.
  In 3, 150 and 151 the transport that failed with "Biological state must have unique
  biological states" (both transport elements bound to one collapsed state) is now
  created: transports 4 -> 5, 4 -> 5, 2 -> 3, messages 7 -> 4, 8 -> 4, 5 -> 1.
- 15 files with no wildcard-bindable state: identical success, messages and created
  counts in every table.
- Not changed: lookup for every other class, find_by_unique_external_id, new_object
  attributes, the owner's element-id branch.

Fix
In store_object, for BiologicalState only, start the lookup hash from
{species_id: nil, tissue_id: nil, cell_type_id: nil, subcellular_location_id: nil} and
merge attribute_hash over it. Components the file gives are matched as before, omitted
ones must be NULL. One statement, no other behaviour change.

Risk
A PWML state that omits a component no longer binds to an existing state that has
it; it binds to the state with that component NULL, or a new row is created. Files
that never relied on that are unaffected (see evidence). pwbs_id is still part of the
lookup, unchanged.

Verify
ruby -c lib/pwml_parser.rb; import a PWML whose states include both (species, location)
and (species, tissue, location) and check that two biological_states rows are created.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 lib/pwml_parser.rb | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/lib/pwml_parser.rb b/lib/pwml_parser.rb
index 88d23c08..845df86d 100644
--- a/lib/pwml_parser.rb
+++ b/lib/pwml_parser.rb
@@ -670,7 +670,17 @@ class PwmlParser
           # duplicate INSERT that collides on that same unique key. Returns nil
           # for anything without such an id, so every other type falls through
           # to the original full-attribute lookup with identical behaviour.
-          existing = find_by_unique_external_id(klass, attribute_hash) || klass.find_by(attribute_hash)
+          # A biological state is identified by its exact (species, tissue, cell type,
+          # subcellular location) tuple, the same rule as its uniqueness validations and
+          # BiologicalState.find_existing. A component the PWML omits (to_hash_shallow
+          # drops blank values) means nil, not any value: without this the omitted key
+          # is missing from find_by and the state binds to an arbitrary sibling that has
+          # that component set.
+          lookup = attribute_hash
+          if klass == BiologicalState
+            lookup = { species_id: nil, tissue_id: nil, cell_type_id: nil, subcellular_location_id: nil }.merge(attribute_hash)
+          end
+          existing = find_by_unique_external_id(klass, attribute_hash) || klass.find_by(lookup)
         end
         debug_pwml("store_object exact lookup result", {
           klass: klass.name,
